# Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true, so a contributor cloning
# there gets a working tree with CRLF. `./test_all.sh` then fails before it
# runs anything. Measured:
#
#   $ sed 's/$/\r/' test_all.sh > t.sh && chmod +x t.sh && ./t.sh
#   env: bash\r: No such file or directory
#
# CI never sees it, because a GitHub runner does not rewrite the tree. The
# person who hits it is a Windows contributor.
* text=auto eol=lf

# These trees are hashed, and the digest is what the two implementations of
# this library promise each other. Letting git decide their bytes would move a
# digest between two checkouts of one commit, and a skill installed from one
# would read as `modified` to the other.
#
# Measured with core.autocrlf=true: a CRLF file under one of these paths is
# stored and checked out with its CRLF, while a file outside them is
# normalised to LF on both. `-text` is what does it. The `eol=lf` above has no
# effect where `text` is unset, whatever `git check-attr` prints.
**/skills/** -text
**/testdata/** -text
